WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.497
A productive industrial hunt starts with environment knowledge.

00:00:04.497 --> 00:00:10.888
Asset inventory should identify systems, locations, owners, functions, criticality, and expected communication.

00:00:10.888 --> 00:00:21.066
A network address becomes meaningful when the hunter knows it is an HMI, controller, historian, engineering workstation, or remote-access gateway.

00:00:21.066 --> 00:00:27.931
A Purdue-style architecture can organize field, control, supervisory, site-operations, industrial-DMZ, and enterprise functions.

00:00:27.931 --> 00:00:32.901
It is a reference rather than a perfect map.

00:00:32.901 --> 00:00:41.659
Hunters need the real zones, conduits, serial and wireless paths, vendor connections, and services crossing each boundary.

00:00:41.659 --> 00:00:45.683
PLCs and RTUs execute or support control.

00:00:45.683 --> 00:00:51.127
HMIs and SCADA platforms provide operator visibility and supervisory authority.

00:00:51.127 --> 00:00:53.731
Historians preserve process trends.

00:00:53.731 --> 00:00:57.282
Engineering workstations hold privileged programming tools.

00:00:57.282 --> 00:01:01.779
Remote-access systems connect support personnel to selected assets.

00:01:01.779 --> 00:01:06.276
Each role changes the significance of observed activity.

00:01:06.276 --> 00:01:13.141
Normal behavior includes expected peers, protocols, timing, commands, user roles, and process modes.

00:01:13.141 --> 00:01:20.478
Startup, shutdown, failover, batch transitions, maintenance, and seasonal operation may all produce different patterns.

00:01:20.478 --> 00:01:26.396
A hunt based on one quiet baseline can misclassify legitimate change.

00:01:26.396 --> 00:01:29.946
Asset criticality and dependency shape scope.

00:01:29.946 --> 00:01:38.704
Unexpected traffic toward a controller may deserve different handling from the same traffic toward a reporting server.

00:01:38.704 --> 00:01:45.095
Hunters should know which systems preserve safety, control, visibility, redundancy, and recovery.

00:01:45.095 --> 00:01:49.593
Operators and engineers supply context that telemetry cannot.

00:01:49.593 --> 00:01:57.877
They can validate work orders, explain process faults, identify unusual equipment behavior, and advise safe escalation.

00:01:57.877 --> 00:02:05.688
Hunters should document assumptions and gaps rather than treating incomplete inventory or architecture as certainty.
